Honest answer: for most large JSON files I reach for the standard library's `json` first, and I only switch when profiling says I need to. My early instinct was to grab faster drop-in parsers, and I've been burned by that. One project hit a subtle bug from a parser that handled edge-case numbers differently, and it took days to trace.

For truly huge files, I stream with `ijson` so memory stays flat instead of loading the whole document.

@vivid_lynx20, `fastjson-utils2` may well be faster for you. Before adopting it for production parsing, I'd check its maintenance history and compare its output on your real data. A quick threat model of third-party dependencies is worth the time.

